Part 1: Downloading & Adding Web-Exploit Rules to Snort 2.9

To make your local Snort installation act like a real Web Application Firewall, you need to feed it rules designed to catch web attacks (SQLi, XSS, Path Traversal).

1. Download the Free Community Ruleset

The Snort team provides a free, public ruleset that contains thousands of production-grade detection signatures. Run this command to download it directly via your terminal:
bash
wget https://snort.org
Use code with caution.
(Note: Even though it says Snort 3, the raw text formatting of the community rules ruleset can be parsed or adapted into Snort 2.9 setups, or you can register a free account on Snort.org to get the specific 2.9 registered user ruleset).

2. Extract and Include Web Rules

If you want to quickly test web rules manually without cluttering your system, you can append them directly to your existing local.rules file:
bash
sudo nano /etc/snort/rules/local.rules
