Part 2: Real-World Payload Mutation (XSS & SQLi Bypass)

Let’s look at exactly how a firewall ruleset stops a basic attack, and how you mutate that attack using Burp Suite or manual encoding to bypass it.

Scenario A: Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)

• The Basic Payload: <script>alert(1)</script>
• The Snort Rule Stopping It:
A typical WAF rule looks for the opening script tag:text
alert tcp any any -> any 80 (msg:"XSS Detected"; content:"<script>"; nocase; sid:1000003;)
Use code with caution.
If you fire the basic payload, Snort matches the string <script> and blocks or logs you.
• The Mutation (The Bypass):
Web applications handle HTML parsing dynamically. You can bypass the signature filter by using alternative HTML event handlers or severe obfuscation that Snort's simple string matching won't recognize, but the browser will still execute:html
<img src=x onerror=alert(1)>
Use code with caution.
Why it works: This payload completely avoids the word <script>, so the firewall lets it through. When it reaches the backend web app and renders on the victim's browser, the broken image triggers the onerror event, executing your JavaScript code anyway.

Scenario B: SQL Injection (SQLi)

• The Basic Payload: 1' UNION SELECT username, password FROM users--
• The Snort Rule Stopping It:
Firewalls often look for the critical sequence of UNION followed closely by SELECT:text
alert tcp any any -> any 80 (msg:"SQLi Union-Select Found"; content:"UNION"; nocase; content:"SELECT"; nocase; sid:1000004;)
Use code with caution.
• The Mutation (The Bypass):
SQL servers are incredibly flexible with how they handle whitespace and characters. You can break up the signature keywords using database comment blocks or unique inline expressions:sql
1'/*!50000UNION*//*!50000SELECT*/username,password FROM users--
Use code with caution.
Or using inline URL hex-encoding if the firewall forgets to decode the layer:text
1'%20%55NION%20%53ELECT%20username...
Use code with caution.
Why it works: Snort looks for the explicit string UNION. Because your payload feeds the firewall /*!50000UNION*/ or %55NION, the firewall rule fails to match the string and marks the request as safe. However, once it reaches a MySQL backend database, the database strips out the /*!50000 */ (which is a comment format meant to execute only on specific MySQL versions) and executes the core query, giving you unauthorized access to the data.

How to use this workflow inside Burp Suite

